shape theory.chemistry.bio.metabolism : theory.chemistry.bio, theory.physics.energy, theory.coherence.law2 {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Metabolism as Structural Transformation.
//
// Metabolism is the transformation law of living systems:
// converting structure between forms while conserving total
// energy (Law 2). Every metabolic reaction is M' = f(C, S)
// where the enzyme is S (structure governing transformation)
// and the substrate is C (character being transformed).
//
// Enzymes are molecular catalysts: forcing shapes (Def 11.1).
// They accelerate resolution between substrates without being
// consumed. They lower the activation energy (the mixing angle
// of the transition state) by providing a contact geometry that
// brings reactants into direct coupling.
//
// ATP (adenosine triphosphate): the energy currency.
//   Three phosphate groups linked by phosphoanhydride bonds.
//   Hydrolysis: ATP -> ADP + Pi releases ~30.5 kJ/mol.
//   This energy is structural: the products (ADP + Pi) have
//   more stable (more coherent) electron configurations than
//   ATP. The difference is available for work.
//   ATP is not "storing energy." It is a high-complexity,
//   low-capacity configuration (high mixing angle). Hydrolysis
//   moves toward lower complexity, higher capacity.
//
// Core metabolic pathways:
//
// Glycolysis: glucose (C6H12O6) -> 2 pyruvate + 2 ATP + 2 NADH.
//   10 enzyme-catalyzed steps. Each step is a specific structural
//   transformation. The pathway is universal across life because
//   it is the most coherent route from glucose to pyruvate.
//
// Citric acid cycle (Krebs): pyruvate -> CO2 + NADH + FADH2 + GTP.
//   8 steps. Completes the oxidation of carbon. Produces electron
//   carriers (NADH, FADH2) for the electron transport chain.
//
// Electron transport chain + oxidative phosphorylation:
//   NADH/FADH2 donate electrons through a series of redox
//   reactions. The energy drives proton pumping across the
//   mitochondrial membrane. The proton gradient (chemiosmosis)
//   drives ATP synthase: a rotary molecular machine that
//   physically rotates to synthesize ATP.
//   ~30-32 ATP per glucose total.
//
// Photosynthesis (in plants/cyanobacteria):
//   Light energy -> electron excitation -> NADPH + ATP ->
//   CO2 fixation (Calvin cycle) -> glucose.
//   This is the reverse: using photon energy (U(1) structure)
//   to drive carbon fixation (increasing molecular complexity).
//   Photosynthesis and respiration are the two directions of the
//   same structural transformation, conserving total energy.
//
// Derives from: theory.chemistry.bio, theory.physics.energy,
//               theory.coherence.law2
  """
}
